  "_note": "Time-on-air vs broadcast-count -- what does 'dwell' actually buy? Cross-run evidence is suggestive but confounded: adv 2000ms x 4s dwell delivered 80% while adv 4000ms x 8s dwell delivered 99%, and both put ~2 broadcasts on air per key -- but they come from different runs, nights and densities. This is a clean 3x2 factorial in ONE run: dwell 4/8/16s crossed with broadcasts-per-key 5/20, adv derived as dwell/broadcasts (800/200, 1600/400, 3200/800 ms). The two factors are fully crossed, so the column effect isolates broadcast count at equal dwell and the row effect isolates dwell at equal broadcast count. 15 reps/condition = 90 cells, 300 keys/condition, 1800 keys, ~4.9h transmit. Analyse with analyze2d.py (rows=update_interval_ms, cols=adv-derived broadcast count) over the resweep.py ground truth; permutation tests on both margins. Expected under the current hypothesis: large monotonic row effect (dwell), flat columns (broadcast count) -> design rule is 'hold keys longer, broadcast as rarely as the power budget likes'. A significant column effect falsifies that.",
